libdjvu/DjVuFile: fail the decoding when its thread cannot be started

DjVuFile::start_decode() sets the DECODING flag and starts a thread that runs
decode_func(), which clears it. The result of GThread::create() was ignored,
so when pthread_create() fails (EAGAIN under RLIMIT_NPROC, or no address space
left for the stack under RLIMIT_AS) the file stays DECODING forever and every
file that includes it waits in wait_for_finish(): ddjvu then waits at 0% CPU
until it is killed. A chain of 300 shared files that each include the next
does this under a 2 GiB address space limit.

Throw DjVuFile.decode_fail when the thread does not start. The handler that
is already there clears DECODING, sets DECODE_FAILED and notifies the ports,
so the including file fails as for any included file that cannot be decoded
and the program reports the error. The life saver reference that the thread
would have released is released here. The thread object that never started
stays in decode_thread, which the next start_decode() and ~DjVuFile() already
delete outside the flags monitor, as the comment above it asks.

That notification must not be sent with the flags monitor of the file held,
which resume_decode() and decode_chunk() did around start_decode(). A page
whose JB2 mask needs a shape dictionary waits for it in get_fgjd() holding the
chunk monitor of each file from the page down to the file it looks at, and
reads the flags of the files below (is_decoding()). The notification takes the
chunk monitor of the files above the one that failed (notify_file_flags_changed()
for DECODE_FAILED), so the two threads waited for each other: ddjvu waited at
0% CPU until it was killed, on a page whose files were drawn when every thread
could start. gdb showed the thread in start_decode() called from
resume_decode() from decode_chunk() waiting for the chunk monitor of a file
that the thread in get_fgjd() held while it waited for the flags of the file
that failed.

start_decode() and resume_decode() now share begin_decode(), which makes the
test that resume_decode() made under a lock of its own (not decoding, not
decoded, not failed) under the one it takes for the start, and decode_chunk()
no longer holds the flags monitor of the included file around the call. The
test and the start stay atomic; the failure is notified with no monitor of the
file held. Nothing else changes for a file whose thread starts.

The two other threads of the library that ddjvu could start ignore the result
of GThread::create() too, and neither waits on a chain of files:
DjVuDocument::start_init() (DjVuDocument.cpp line 208) starts the one thread
that reads the directory of a document when ddjvu opens it, before any file of
the document is created, and ddjvu_runnablejob_s::start() (ddjvuapi.cpp line
2695) starts the jobs of ddjvu_document_save() and ddjvu_document_print(),
which ddjvu does not run.

